# They Must Be Ashamed!
Tamil title: வெட்கப்பட வேண்டும்!

They had lived behind prison bars. With honour they chanted Gandhi’s sacred name. They suffered British repression. Possessing the courage to forgo property and comfort and remain separated from kith and kin, at least for a time, they joined the struggle for national freedom. Their names were engraved upon the roll of the martyrs who won us liberty.

There are many kinds among these martyrs. We must never forget the good people who expected no reward, were moved by a sense of duty, and obeyed instructions while accepting hardship and loss in the rare task of removing the country’s disgrace and breaking the chains of slavery. Nor should their sacrifice be undervalued. Only people of blemished minds would make such calculations and rejoice. These martyrs deserve the people’s praise and acclaim forever.

There is another kind. They too went to prison, and it is true that they made sacrifices. Yet there is a vast difference between the sacrifice of the former group and what is said to have been done by these people. Once the prison doors opened, falling garlands, public receptions, and popular praise did not satisfy them. They had no concern for how the people’s lives might be transformed into prosperous ones, what the best methods were, what measures other countries had adopted to dispel darkness from their people’s lives, or how those measures might be introduced here to remove ignorance, poverty, and premature death.

Their entire concern was to discover which methods would most quickly achieve their chosen ends: converting into money the influence they acquired among the public through imprisonment; finding government jobs for relatives close to them; and crushing former comrades who had once served with purity of thought, word, and deed.

The people cannot distinguish at the outset between the worthy and the unworthy. Even in countries where literacy has spread among most people, that is not always possible. It is futile to expect such advanced discernment from the majority of the people of the Indian subcontinent.

The educated are very few here, and those who have received a worldly education that develops reason are fewer still. Our people cannot grasp the truth that all the troubles afflicting us arose from acts committed by some among us to fulfil their private desires, rather than from causes beyond human knowledge.

Our people have not yet understood the lesson taught by the electric lamp that shines like the sun, the train that carries them within hours to a city once reached only after days of walking, and countless similar inventions. Their intellect remains dimmed to that extent. If only ordinary people were in this condition, it would be one thing; the ministers who govern are of the same character. Did our ministers not name an aeroplane purchased from America “Hanuman”? Ministers suited to the people have been found!

To return to the point: the uplifting confidence that “we too can accomplish it” has still not arisen among our people. They must acquire confidence that their present troubles are the results of previous actions and that, if corrective measures are taken today, they can see a realm of happiness in the future. Those who work to create that confidence truly serve the people. Abandoning this task to speak of epic grandeur, the history of the *Kanda Puranam*, the glory of the *Garuda Puranam*, and the greatness of sacred ash may help fatten one’s own flesh, but it will not raise future generations.

Today all seven great provinces of the Indian subcontinent are ruled by “martyrs.” Every minister in office was made to spend at least some time in prison. They were seated upon the throne of authority as a sign of the favourable opinion people held of them and because of popular faith in their sacrifices.

However loudly and repeatedly those among the people who possess the franchise declare today’s rule to be “people’s rule,” those who understand the actual condition will never accept it as democracy. Only thirteen out of every hundred people now have the right to choose their representatives. The remaining eighty-seven possess no vote. Under these circumstances, how can it be fitting to call the system and those adorning the seats of power democratic, or to describe the ministry as one possessing popular influence?

Democracy cannot mean merely counting heads. As the world-renowned thinker Shaw said, must democracy not be determined by what is inside those heads? Even if judged without that progressive principle, today’s system is not popular rule even by ordinary democratic standards.

Take the Madras Legislature as an example. There are two chambers, called the Upper House and the Lower House. It is supposedly there that the representatives of the poor and humble sit. The chamber has 215 members. Apart from Muslim members and one or two independents, all belong to the ministerial party, the Congress. Most went to prison; one or two obtained their chairs by carrying the kavadi for Kamaraj.

Each member receives a monthly salary of 130 rupees, travel expenses for attending legislative sittings, and a daily allowance during the session. Having received all this, they render “service” to the people. Voters may have thought of all manner of matters unrelated to themselves when voting for these men. Believing the sacrifices publicised in their names, they may have entrusted their votes to them to demonstrate that they were not ungrateful. Does this not reveal the voters’ immense love, affection, and attachment towards the martyrs?

How should legislators behave towards people who treated them with such love? Should they not act so that voters retain not even a mustard seed of distrust? Should they not devote all their time to removing the voters’ grievances and satisfying their needs? Have they done so thus far?

Whenever the Legislature meets, should they not postpone private business and work for others for a few days and carefully examine every issue: what new law is proposed, what its effects will be, whether it will cause harm or benefit, and whether it can give the public a comfortable life? If a law harms the majority, should they not oppose and defeat it? If that is impossible, should they not at least explain its injuries to their constituents, show why and how the harmful law is being introduced, secure the people’s consent, and only then vote on the resolution? Only representatives with the dignity to respect people generally and their voters particularly would possess such wisdom.

Suppose a bill raising sales tax or imposing another new levy is introduced. What is a legislator’s duty? Is it not proper and orderly to consult the people in his district who will be affected? Has anyone heard of even one member doing so? Can anyone confidently say they ever contemplated it?

Even after a bill affecting people’s lives has become law and entered the statute book, have they met their constituents and explained, “The Legislature passed this law this month. It will confer these particular benefits and improve your future life”? They have not done even that.

Perhaps one ought not expect such “small matters” from these great men. A schoolboy may skip school, spend school hours playing elsewhere or sleeping on someone’s veranda, and return when the other children come home. So long as his parents do not discover it, he escapes. Once they learn the truth, will they welcome him by saying, “Come, darling! What are all those other children good for? They attend school properly and study their lessons, the useless creatures! Would they acquire wisdom even if they drank the water in which your feet were washed?” No. They will beat him with whatever comes to hand. Nor will they stop there; they will shower him with names such as “veranda sleeper,” “great lump,” and “rice-fed ox.”

These legislators ought to behave with more responsibility than a schoolchild. They enjoy the trust of many and determine people’s daily arrangements. Yet do they perform even their assigned task with care and attention? They do not.

Except for one or two, all Congress members invariably enter the Legislature during sessions and mark themselves “present” in the register. But while the House is actually sitting, they are absent from the hall. They give it the slip and amuse themselves in China Bazaar, or rather Netaji Bazaar, Mount Road, and elsewhere. Some remain in their hotels, eat delicious meals, enjoy a sound sleep, and prepare themselves to see a cinema at night. Others spend their time buying toys for their children and soap, powder, ribbon, and other household articles. It is shameful.

Suppose a salaried official does not perform assigned work properly and on time. Would the minister authorised to control him let the official proceed as he pleased? Would he not angrily demand an explanation? A warning first, suspension for several days second, and dismissal in the end: are these not the measures imposed upon a disorderly official?

If even an ordinary official bears such responsibility and faces such strict consequences for neglecting duty, should not legislators who receive travel expenses, daily allowance, and monthly salary from public taxes bear still greater responsibility? Can they forget that the money for which they stretch out their hands was amassed as tax by common people turning their blood into sweat?

These people sit uselessly in the legislative hall after winning popular affection and support. They gladly collect the people’s tax money month after month and squander it like fireworks. Have they ever asked whether, even if they failed to provide a good life in return, they at least refrained from harming the people?

Whatever benefit or welfare they have produced must be explained to those who elected them, especially because those voters chose them out of abundant affection without weighing their qualifications. That is their foremost duty, the one indispensable good they owe their voters. Have these legislative statues ever remembered it?

Suppose villagers one day ask: “You took our votes. What have you achieved for us? We saw you then, speaking to us with melting sweetness; afterwards you never looked our way. Where have you been all this time? We had a house and a little farmland. During these five years we sold everything to settle our debts. Rising prices caused it. We knew no other way to save our family. We sought you to ask about the price rise and request action, but could not find you. Have you come today because you respect us? Conscienceless men! Men without humanity! Fine people who repay goodness with ingratitude! Go away!” What answer will these ministers and schemers, and the mute dolls who support them, give? Must they not be ashamed?

“My Complaint is that they do not put enough questions to me in the Legislature and elicit information regarding various aspects of the Finance Department.”

That is the charge: not enough questions are asked in the Legislature to obtain from the minister the necessary explanations concerning the affairs of the Finance Department. Who issued this testimonial to the legislators? An opponent of Congress? Some worthless man ignorant of sacrifice? Not at all. It was the “certificate” given to his supporting colleagues by the Honourable Gopal Reddy, Finance Minister in the Madras government.

“They do not ask enough questions to clarify matters.” Who fails to ask them? The legislators. Who makes the accusation? A minister belonging to the same group. Even after this, the members feel no shame.

If they cannot understand an ordinary matter, how will they understand highly complex economics? They understand only one thing: raising their hands. At month’s end, they understand one more: stretching out those hands for payment. Apart from these two, nothing enters their understanding or their ears.

At the same banquet, Upper House member Dr A. Lakshmanaswami Mudaliar reportedly mocked the Lower House: while fifty percent of Upper House members took part in the Madras government budget debate, not even twenty-five percent of Lower House members participated.

Exclude the no fewer than thirty-five Muslim League members of the Lower House, and the percentage among the remaining Congress members who joined the debate becomes smaller still. Whom should we blame for this disgraceful condition? Both the men who merely decorate the chamber and the voters who sent them there without understanding their quality must be ashamed.

At times the Legislature lacks even the quorum necessary to conduct a sitting. The presiding officer ringing the bell until his hand aches has become a major feature of its proceedings. What devotion to duty! What responsibility!

That is the story of the members. The ministers’ story scarcely needs telling. The Premier dislikes the Legislature itself. Since he devotes his complete attention to the administrative affairs of the Madras government, he never even looks towards the legislative chamber. Previously the Honourable Subbarayan answered questions there on the Premier’s behalf. When some portion of his “sacrifice” was somehow found deficient, he was pushed from office. The Honourable Gopal Reddy now performs that sacred service.

The Legislature met on the eleventh of this month. Instead of the customary twenty questions, only seven were reportedly asked. Do you know why? People were present to ask, but no one was there to answer. Of the twelve ministers, all except the three Andhra ministers had gone touring outside the city. The Leader of the House, the Honourable Gopal Reddy, was also away.

Had these ministers understood the dignity and nature of their positions, would they have travelled while the Legislature was in session? Their duty to the people lay in the chamber. Yet they abandoned the place where that duty had to be performed and went elsewhere to indulge in idle talk, receive honours, and enjoy excursions. No one except these “martyrs” could even conceive of such conduct.

The Premier is said to have requested and warned ministers not to leave town during legislative sessions. The ministers remain deaf.

Gandhi’s sacred name helped them earlier, as did the sacrifice they are said to have made. What will help make them ministers again? They must tell the people what they achieved while in office. Though many may place the burden upon God and remain silent maidens, at least some will angrily demand, “Sir, tell us what you achieved.”

Will they speak of the salary they received and continue to receive?

Of travel allowances exceeding even that salary?

Of the jobs they secured for relatives?

Of the prosperity they created for themselves?

Of their improper attempt to impose Hindi and raze Tamil life to the ground?

Of the baton blows and prison sentences inflicted upon those who opposed them?

Will their silence convey: “Then we ruled arrogantly, saying the Indian Penal Code would suppress your actions, the police would subdue you, and the army would run at our call if you overstepped; today, behold, we stand before you”?

What are they going to say? After some days pass, what can be expected from them except to bow their heads in shame, lose their words, and stand exhausted?

In the future, will only the voters who gave them this life corner them with questions? Every person without a vote who pays sales tax will question them too. While they still possess power, let them imagine what the public will think and how mockingly people will speak after that power is lost. Today, because they sit enthroned upon seats of authority, such a mental picture will only provoke anger and the thought, “Shall we call the police and send them to suppress these people?”

They should turn the pages that tell how many emperors, who despised the people, magnified authority, and ruled the earth with armies as their support, were levelled during their own lifetimes even while guarding themselves with every possible precaution. The public is blazing fire; these men are mere dry leaves.

One day people gathered before the home of the ruler Cromwell, clamouring that they wished to see him. His commanders asked, “Cromwell, do you see how much enthusiasm the people have for you?” Cromwell replied, “Is this remarkable? When they come to punish me, a larger multitude will display still greater enthusiasm.”

If a person is intoxicated by the glitter of acquired power and conducts himself without humanity, the very power that once provided his comfort will ultimately become his executioner.

Those who obtain people’s votes and take office, yet do no good for the people and treat them with contempt, will shrink from facing them after power slips from their hands. A time will come when they must be ashamed.

(Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 26 September 1948.)

---NOTES---
The article distinguishes principled freedom fighters from politicians who converted imprisonment and nationalist prestige into office, patronage, and personal gain.
“காவடி எடுத்து” literally means carrying a kavadi; here it denotes conspicuous, servile support offered to gain political favour.
“திரிகரண சுத்தி” means purity in thought, speech, and action.
The English quotation attributed to Finance Minister Gopal Reddy was unreadable OCR noise. It was restored verbatim from scan `008-722d3c69e5.png` before translation.
“பிரதமர்” refers in this provincial context to the Premier of Madras, not the Prime Minister of India.
The source’s Cromwell anecdote is retained as presented; its historical attribution is not independently corrected within the source text.
